\section{Recursion Over Symbols}

In his 1960 paper \textit{Recursive Functions of Symbolic Expressions and
Their Computation by Machine, Part I}, John McCarthy changed the domain of
the familiar recursion problem. Instead of computing just with numbers,
the functions would decompose and construct symbolic expressions. An
S-expression is an atom or a pair of S-expressions. A proper list is a
chain of pairs whose second components eventually reach the distinguished
empty list. Thus \verb|(A B)| abbreviates \verb|(A . (B . NIL))|,
whereas \verb|(A . B)| is a pair but not a proper list. A list was a
convenient case of a recursively defined data type, not a limit on what
could be built with pairs.

Gary D. Knott's \textit{Interpreting LISP} develops the distinction
between atoms and nonatomic S-expressions before implementing the
interpreter. That order matters: a recursive routine over a proper
list needs only an empty case and a tail case, while one traversing
an arbitrary finite S-expression must be prepared to recurse into
both fields of a pair. The appropriate recursion comes from the data
domain, not the way parentheses are indented.

McCarthy's retrospective \textit{History of Lisp} singles out symbolic
differentiation as a problem that pulled the pieces together. A derivative
routine can examine the outer operator and recurse on operands, but only
after deciding which case applies. A strict ordinary function with
arguments for both alternatives cannot implement that decision: it would
already have tried to evaluate the recursive case on an atom. Herbert
Stoyan's \textit{Early LISP History (1956--1959)} sets these decisions
against the preceding experiments in list processing. Church's
$\lambda$ notation supplied a way to write an anonymous function; it did
not supply Lisp's concrete data model or make Lisp evaluation identical
to unrestricted $\beta$-reduction in Chapter~\ref{ch:untyped-lambda}.

\section{The Evaluator and Its Boundary}

The paper distinguishes the notation for writing function definitions
from the symbolic structures that can represent them. The key step is to
choose a representation in which the evaluator can recognize an operator
and traverse its operands with the same pair operations available to
other programs. Paul Graham's \textit{The Roots of Lisp} retraces this
construction. The point is not that every list is a program: the same
pair structure can be treated as data or evaluated as a form, depending
on which operation receives it.

A useful abstract interface is $\operatorname{eval}(e,\rho)$ and
$\operatorname{apply}(f,\vec v,\rho)$, with $e$ a form, $\rho$ a binding
environment and $\vec v$ a sequence of argument values. In a small Lisp
evaluator, a variable in expression position is looked up; \texttt{quote}
returns its operand untouched; a conditional evaluates tests until one
succeeds and evaluates only the selected consequent. An ordinary
application obtains argument values before applying the function.
McCarthy's paper gives a universal symbolic function using these ideas.
For an ordinary direct function call, the paper's evaluator adds
parameter bindings to the current association list. Steven S. Muchnick
and Uwe F. Pleban's \textit{A Semantic Comparison of Lisp and Scheme}
shows why modern lexical closures require a different rule: application
extends the environment saved at the function's definition, not the
caller's environment. Without this distinction, a local variable in
the caller can accidentally change the meaning of the callee.

\section{Worked Example: A Branch That Must Not Run}

Consider the following fragment in a modern Common Lisp spelling, with
\texttt{car} and \texttt{cdr} used only on a proper list.

\begin{verbatim}
(defun keep-a (xs)
  (cond ((null xs) nil)
        ((eq (car xs) 'a) (cons 'a (keep-a (cdr xs))))
        (t (keep-a (cdr xs)))))
(keep-a '(b a a)) ; => (A A)
\end{verbatim}

The initial quote causes the reader's list structure to be used as data;
\texttt{b} does not have to name a function. At the first call the
second test fails, so only the final clause recurses. The next two calls
each add one \texttt{a} to the result after recursion returns.
The base case must return without evaluating any later clause, or even
the empty input may fail to terminate. The invariant is that the return
value is the subsequence of \texttt{a} symbols in the current suffix;
the suffix length decreases in every recursive call. David S. Touretzky's
\textit{Common Lisp: A Gentle Introduction to Symbolic Computation} teaches
recursion through templates and traces of this form, and makes it possible
to separate a recursion error from a reader or binding error.
Anderson, Farrell, and Sauers's study \textit{Learning to Program in
LISP} examined novice problem-solving protocols; tracing the concrete
subproblem addressed by each call is useful when an apparently simple
recursive definition hides the intermediate goals from a new reader.

An evaluator defined in the language it evaluates makes the description
compact, but leaves a bootstrap question: something must execute the
first copy. Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-152} follows that step onto actual
hardware, and Chapter~13 of \textit{The Modern Compiler Dossier}
distinguishes the analogous self-hosting construction from a proof that
its output is correct.
